// An array that contains itself.
//
// `ring.push(ring)` stores the array's own pointer into the array. With
// retain-on-copy that raises the count by one, so the array now holds a
// reference to itself: the count cannot reach zero while the array is live,
// and it never does — the array is leaked when the program ends.
//
// That leak is *accepted* (docs/memory-model.md §6.1): a cyclic aggregate is a
// documented, bounded-in-kind programmer-visible leak, not a bug. What must
// NOT happen is a memory error: the array must stay readable through the
// cycle, and nothing may be freed while it is still reachable.
//
// So this probe asserts the safety property, not the reclamation:
//
//   1. the values read back through the cycle are the ones that were written
//   2. the program runs to completion
//   3. ASan and UBSan report no memory error
//
// A cycle that *was* reclaimed would be the bug: that would mean the count
// reached zero with the array still reachable, and every read through it
// would be a use-after-free.
//
// Expected: 4 / 3 / 7

fn main() {
    mut ring: [[int64]] = [[0], [1, 2, 3], [7, 8, 9]];
    // The array now contains a pointer to itself.
    ring.push(ring);

    println(ring.len);
    // Read *through* the cycle: ring[3] is the array itself, so ring[3][1]
    // is ring[1], which is [1, 2, 3] — and its last element is 3.
    println(ring[3][1][2]);
    // The original elements are still readable, unchanged.
    println(ring[2][0]);
}
